  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 AM MDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nTropical Storm Rachel is making an approach to Southern\nCalifornia/Baja today, aided by a Pacific shortwave aloft merging\nwith the system and accelerating it to the northeast. Locally,\nsouth flow ahead of this system is pulling up moisture.\nPrecipitable water values are expected to jump to around 1.0\" by\nmid-afternoon. Weakly unstable profile combined with stronger\nforcing aloft should result in isolated showers and thunderstorms\nacross Southwest New Mexico in the late afternoon and evening\nhours tonight. Storms will be weak, but could produce some gusty\nwinds. HRRR has a slight chance of rain showers as far east as El\nPaso around sunset tonight, but chances are slim.\n\nIncreased PoPs for Sunday compared to blended guidance. Right\nentrance region of a 75-knot 500mb jet streak will aid in the \ndevelopment of scattered thunderstorms across Southwest New Mexico\nSunday afternoon. A few storms may be capable of severe wind \ngusts, given 0-6km shear around 40 knots and DCAPE 1000-1200 J/kg.\nWarm nose aloft on model soundings will hinder storm coverage, but\nI do think there's enough forcing for storm chances. HRRR also\nshows another wave late Sunday night, bringing rain showers nearly\narea-wide. This will be worth monitoring for localized flooding\nrisk.\n\nRemnants of Simon begin to approach Monday/Tuesday. Models are\ngetting more and more confident that this moisture will miss us \nto the east. Token PoPs still in place across Hudspeth County, but\nchances for dry weather are higher across the majority of the\nforecast area.\n\nDry air within the base of the Pacific low progressing over the \nCentral Rockies will flush out moisture locally and lead to a \nstreak of dry weather Wednesday through the rest of the week \nahead. Pattern is favorable to expect a backdoor cold front and \ncooler temperatures Friday into next weekend. Global models \nstruggle with the evolution of this Pacific low. ECMWF detaches it\nfrom the polar jet and retrogrades it over the area, GFS \nprogresses it within a stronger jet flow. Leaning toward the GFS\nsolution, the drier and cooler solution.",
